    "knowledge_point_id": "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity",
    "problem": "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=5,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(-2)^{2}+2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=3,$ while $f(-2)=5.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$",
